 updates=[('A print shop has $300 of fixed weekly cost and $4 of variable cost per poster. If 50 posters are made, what is C(50) in dollars?','Add the fixed cost once: C(50)=300+4(50).','Correct. The $500 total includes $300 fixed cost and $200 variable cost.'),('A print shop sells every poster made for $12 each. What revenue, R(50), comes from 50 sales? Enter dollars.','Revenue is price times number sold: R(50)=12(50).','Correct. Revenue is $600 before subtracting any costs.'),('An event has fixed cost $320, price $12 per item, and variable cost $4 per item. Assuming every item made is sold, find the exact break-even quantity.','Set P(q)=(12−4)q−320=0 and solve for q.','Correct. At 40 items, revenue and cost are both $480, so profit is zero.'),('In a fixed-price linear model, each item sells for $15 and costs $9 to make. How much does profit increase with one additional item made and sold? Enter dollars.','One additional sale adds $15 revenue and $9 cost. Their difference is marginal profit.','Correct. Each extra sale increases profit by $6, including while total profit is still negative.'),('Assume fixed cost is positive, price and variable cost are constant, and p>v. For quantities greater than the exact break-even quantity, what is true?','Beyond the crossing, the steeper revenue line is above the cost line. Compare both at the same quantity.','Correct. Revenue exceeds cost there, so modeled profit is positive.')]
